{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-160607" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u(u+v),\\,y=\\frac{2u}{v+1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-160607" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u(u+v),\\,\\frac{2u}{v+1}) \\\\  & =(u(u+0),\\,\\frac{2u}{0+1}) \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,2u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx={u}^{2},\\,y=2u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2},\\,y(t)=2t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\nx={(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{2}=4x\n$$\n\nwhich is a right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{1}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,0)\\to (0(0+0),\\,\\frac{2(0)}{0+1})=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(1,0)\\to (1(1+0),\\,\\frac{2(1)}{0+1})=(1,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{1}$ is an arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/4fc3c209e83fc1d6b4c9258cdb739fb0a73db797791cf8fcca2e51a7592d54f8.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-160607" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8930f6ede5f77a7bdafd56730621ff05cedfd00dba5a32b1a520477e4ae18962"} {"question" "q-160607" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-160607" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2).$"] ["text" "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,2)$ to $(0,0).$"] ["text" "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from  $(1,0)$ to $(2,1).$"] ["text" "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2).$"] ["text" "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(2,1)$ to $(1,0).$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-160607" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2).$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-160632" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,y=u.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-160632" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{0}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a right-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({1}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,1) &  \\\\  & =(1+{v}^{2},\\,1), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,1)$ to the point $(2,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{1}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+1,\\,u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a right-opening parabola traversed from the point $(2,1)$ to the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({0}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,0) &  \\\\  & =({v}^{2},\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/56f11a4e05de6f090f94d38550b4cd21f042c1acc5d9ae67242de5fe3e9c2e50.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-160632" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8930f6ede5f77a7bdafd56730621ff05cedfd00dba5a32b1a520477e4ae18962"} {"question" "q-160632" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-160632" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/19650cf966c3c455fa859877b4718c08aea8a343876e5817c625618098a84730.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/565227489ba6ae7a657a4b2f1e0133bc117e0e666557f58f59ec0272ad8c574e.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/56f11a4e05de6f090f94d38550b4cd21f042c1acc5d9ae67242de5fe3e9c2e50.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/9dc7559632cbecf450389f362ca478d40576ac9646489fda281519c48f49eb16.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/eb6908aea50ad1c314efefdaaf922a8c637673a38f76ee8aadcbdc361f44ff78.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-160632" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/56f11a4e05de6f090f94d38550b4cd21f042c1acc5d9ae67242de5fe3e9c2e50.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-225035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v-u,\\,y={v}^{3}+u.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-225035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v-u,\\,{v}^{3}+u) \\\\  & =(0-u,\\,{0}^{3}+u) \\\\  & =(-u,u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=-u,\\,y=u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=-t,\\,y(t)=t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(-1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/fc5daa3ee483ed2a7e78d30a247c6d4c9ae4d77691d5d5659c0b9ac2fdf6621a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-225035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8930f6ede5f77a7bdafd56730621ff05cedfd00dba5a32b1a520477e4ae18962"} {"question" "q-225035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-225035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-1,0)$ and $(0,-1)$"] ["text" "$(-1,1)$ and $(0,0)$"] ["text" "$(0,-1)$ and $(-1,0)$"] ["text" "$(0,0)$ and $(-1,1)$"] ["text" "$(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-225035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(0,0)$ and $(-1,1)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-225150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=2v(u-1),\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}v(4-u).\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-225150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(2v(u-1),\\,\\frac{1}{2}v(4-u)) \\\\  & =(2v(0-1),\\,\\frac{1}{2}v(4-0)) \\\\  & =(-2v,2v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=-2v,\\,y=2v,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=-2t,\\,y(t)=2t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(-2,2)$ and $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/e6ef0f52fbe6638659dd7b7f3d702d4bfbb07b83a7f7742c9b0822157908b706.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-225150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8930f6ede5f77a7bdafd56730621ff05cedfd00dba5a32b1a520477e4ae18962"} {"question" "q-225150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-225150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-2,2)$ and $(0,0)$"] ["text" "$(0,0)$ and $(-2,2)$"] ["text" "$(0,0)$ and $(2,-2)$"] ["text" "$(0,2)$ and $(-2,0)$"] ["text" "$(2,-2)$ and $(0,0)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-225150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(-2,2)$ and $(0,0)$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-225168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u+v-2,\\,y={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-225168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u+v-2,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) \\\\  & =(u+1-2,\\,{u}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(u-1,\\,{u}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=u-1,\\,y={u}^{2}-1,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t-1,\\,y(t)={t}^{2}-1,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\ny={(x+1)}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nwhich is an upward-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(-1,-1).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{3}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(1,1)\\to (1+1-2,\\,{1}^{2}-{1}^{2})=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(0,1)\\to (0+1-2,\\,{0}^{2}-{1}^{2})=(-1,-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{3}$ is an arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(-1,-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/assets/b139c5b531191bc8aafbb14ad261989ef374503c63f54a567e695e287d4359be.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-225168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8930f6ede5f77a7bdafd56730621ff05cedfd00dba5a32b1a520477e4ae18962"} {"question" "q-225168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-225168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(-1,0)$ to $(0,1).$"] ["text" "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,0).$"] ["text" "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(-1,-1)$ to $(0,0).$"] ["text" "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(-1,-1)$ to $(1,1).$"] ["text" "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(-1,-1).$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063756/state.json" "file_sha256" "4de165348f3f4e3225aedd2c4817ccd2938a0a29683f9fcacb346dc12154f5fd"} {"question" "q-225168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(-1,-1).$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_A